<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesud I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1156">1156</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Masud I of Rum, Rukn al-Din Mesud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mesud I was sultan of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> from 1116 to 1156, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-i">Kilij Arslan I</a>. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> the settled capital of a reunited state, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> in 1147 his forces routed the German army of Conrad III near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a>. His long reign consolidated the sultanate in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesud-i.jpg" alt="Mesud I" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rukn_al-Din_Mas%27ud_I._AH_510-551_(AD_1116-1156)_Half-length_facing_bust_of_Byzantine_emperor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mesud was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-i">Kilij Arslan I</a>, who died fighting Ridwan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur River</a> in 1107. He was passed over for the throne in favour of his brother Malik Shah. After Malik Shah was defeated by the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> at the Battle of Philomelion in 1116, Mesud rebelled. With the help of his father-in-law, the Danishmend ruler Gümüshtigin, he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> later that year and defeated Malik Shah, who was blinded and eventually murdered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Mesud later turned on the Danishmends and conquered some of their lands. In 1130 he began building the Alâeddin Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, which was completed in 1221. In 1146 he fended off a Byzantine attack on his capital. Near the end of his reign he fought the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>, one led by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany">Conrad III of Germany</a> and the other by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a>. He defeated the first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a>, near modern Eskişehir, in 1147, and the second at Laodicea, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denizli">Denizli</a>, in 1148. In September 1149 he invaded the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and captured Marash.</p><p>Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> persuaded him to attack Thoros II and demand his submission. An attack in 1154, provoked by an Armenian raid into Seljuk lands in Cappadocia, was routed by Thoros with a contingent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>. The Armenian chronicler Smbat Sparapet describes the sultan's army fleeing with its horses dying. Mesud died in 1156 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a>. His daughter Kamero married John Tzelepes Komnenos, a Komnenos who had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mesud I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kilij Arslan rebuilt his state around Konya, which became the permanent capital under his son <b>Mesud I</b>, and in 1101 his army destroyed the crusading expeditions that followed in the wake of the First Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
